    <h1 class="text-page-title">PNW Radon Gas Testing &amp; Sub-Slab Mitigation</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Understanding indoor radon risks in King and Snohomish counties: why Western Washington is classified as low-risk EPA Zone 3, when testing is actually necessary, and how to access free state testing resources.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Why Most Puget Sound Buyers Can Skip the $300 Radon Inspection</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Don't waste funds on unnecessary inspection fees. King and Snohomish counties are officially rated EPA Zone 3 (Low Risk), meaning natural indoor radon levels across Seattle, Shoreline, Lynnwood, and Edmonds are overwhelmingly low. Unless you're purchasing a home with a deep, unventilated basement carved directly into foothill glacial moraines, a routine $300 radon inspection contingency is rarely warranted on standard Puget Sound homes.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Radon in Western Washington: Geology &amp; EPA Zone 3</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Radon is an odorless, colorless radioactive soil gas formed by the natural decay of trace uranium in deep bedrock. Because it creeps upward through soil pores, it can enter building foundations through slab cracks, expansion joints, or unsealed crawl spaces.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      In Washington State, elevated radon levels are heavily concentrated in Eastern Washington (Spokane, Stevens, and Ferry counties) due to granite-rich geology. By contrast, the Puget Sound basin consists primarily of marine clay and glaciomarine drift. Consequently, the EPA designates both King and Snohomish counties as Zone 3, with predicted average indoor screening levels below 2.0 pCi/L—well below the federal action threshold of 4.0 pCi/L.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">When Does Radon Testing Make Sense?</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      While regional baseline risk is low, specific structural designs and localized Cascade foothill soil pockets warrant targeted evaluation.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Zone 3 Lowlands</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Crawl Space &amp; Slab Homes</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Homes across Seattle, Lynnwood, Shoreline, and Edmonds built on ventilated crawl spaces or slabs on grade carry exceptionally low risk. Routine purchase contingencies are unnecessary unless desired for personal peace of mind.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Foothill Soils</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Deep Cascade Basements</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Homes in foothill communities (Monroe, Sultan, Gold Bar, North Bend) featuring deep subterranean living spaces carved into glacial till warrant a simple 48-hour short-term test or a free test kit from the state.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Active Systems</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Homes with Mitigation</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          If a home already features an active sub-slab depressurization pipe and inline exhaust fan, verify that the U-tube manometer liquid shows active suction pressure and conduct routine re-testing every two years.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Testing Options &amp; Sub-Slab Mitigation</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Testing and permanent mitigation are inexpensive and highly accessible across Washington State without high commercial fees.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">State Health Program</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Free WA DOH Test Kits</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          The Washington State Department of Health Radon Program provides free DIY test canisters by mail to state residents. Place the canister in your lowest living space for 3 to 7 days and mail it with prepaid postage.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Rapid Screening</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Hardware Store DIY Kits</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Short-term charcoal absorption test kits are readily available at local hardware retailers for $15 to $25. They provide quick screening data within days if immediate escrow verification is desired.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Permanent Engineering Fix</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Sub-Slab Depressurization</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          If testing reveals radon above 4.0 pCi/L, installing an active sub-slab mitigation system (PVC suction pit and inline exhaust fan) costs between $1,500 and $2,500, permanently exhausting soil gas above the roofline.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

